<p>Part One runs from the book's printed page 2 through page 13: two short stories, two pages of family photographs, six further stories (one, "T-Bone," illustrated with a reproduced 1943 Colorado motor vehicle title), a page of World War II photographs, and two more stories, ending with "Curiodditie s." This site's own part division is noted in the book's introduction on the main page for "Hell Cats And She Ghosts."</p>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="pink-and-blue-booties">Pink and Blue Booties</h2>

  <p>Some seventy years age, before hi-tech and equal rights activists, little girls could be little girls and develop their imaginations, little boys could be little boys and develop into little hellions. Little girls had their imaginary tea parties and the stork brought little dolls that just fit into little baby buggies to be paraded around so adults could "ooh" and "aah" over the rubber dolls with the glass eyes and tell them, "Oh my, it looks just like you," then ask, "Is it a boy or a girl?" The little girl doesn't have the faintest idea because the stork brought it and he didn't tell her if it was a boy or a girl. So...mothers came up with the idea of pink booties for girls and blue booties for boys to solve this embarrassing problem.</p>

  <p>Little boys were more interested in frogs and snakes and learning how to smoke without choking or getting sick. They couldn't have cared less about the sex of play dolls in play baby buggies. They were aware that the stork had brought them, personally, from his baby factory and that their dads were going to shoot the damned stork but they had never seen him and presumed it came in the night. If it brought those little play babies, too, that was beyond their comprehension though they felt the stork and Santa Claus must be in cahoots. Whatever or however, they knew the stork was a very busy bird and if he didn't slow down he would be filled full of buckshot.</p>

  <p>That imaginary world was a wonderful place to be. If something about it wasn't quite right you could blink your eyes and change it. When my mother needed my stick horse to sweep the kitchen I could crawl into the Essex sedan with my cousin Lee and tour the country. It was on jacks, didn't have gasoline or a battery and chickens roosted on the seats but that didn't deter us until Lee tried to cross the railroad tracks and a train hit us. We rolled out of it to safety as chickens squawked and Aunt Caroline ran out to see why we were laying stretched out on either side, unmoving. It took a whole lot of imagination to put it back together after that wreck and it never did steer right again.</p>

  <p>We grew older and the little girls who had been our age and had tea parties were now older than us. We had reached fourteen years of age with changes in basic metabolism that made the smell of perfume delectable, the addition of curves and bumps on the girls who had wanted us to play house with them eight years before noticeable and alluring. We couldn't understand this change however we were comfortable with it and were ready to play house. Our uncles had told us the story of the stork was a myth, if you wanted a baby you took a box of candy to a girl and let nature take it's course. We learned that advice was a myth, too, because there are no fourteen through sixteen age girls. When they reach puberty they blink their eyes and, suddenly, they are seventeen and eighteen years old. That took more imagination than putting the old Essex sedan back together after a train demolished it.</p>

  <p>Clifton "Lee" Davis was born in Creede, Colorado. He enlisted in the U.S. Marine Corps in 1943 at the age of 17 years. He was wounded in battle on Saipan Island of the Marianas. He returned to Creede where he mined for years with his brother, Frankie, and, later, they ran "The Pick &amp; Shovel Rock Shop". Lee died in 1997 of congestive heart failure. We were best friends as well as cousins and explored our small world with a derring-do that equalled feats of our heroes and surely brought grey hairs to the heads of our mothers.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 2. "Some seventy years age" for "ago" is printed as shown. This story's mention of Lee's 1997 death is the source of an earlier, superseded guess that the book was printed "after 1997"; "Curiodditie s," later in Part Two, gives the firmer date of 2001 or later.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="snakes-alive">Snakes Alive</h2>

  <p>"The Ranch", that was all we ever called it, a homestead of 160 acres at the mouth of Sawmill Gulch stretching to within 300 yards of the Rio Grande to the north and abutting the Barber place to the west. First recorded in Chester Garnier's name, for whom the next gulch west was named, then sold to Carl Gilbreath who, in turn sold out to John Dabney.</p>

  <p>The ranch, as we knew it in the 1920s was in the hands of our grandparents, Charles and Maude Lane, who had a firewood and mine prop business, sold milk and eggs to Creede residents, raised hay for a trio of horses and three milk cows, raised vegetables and helped raise grandchildren. I was a grandchild who loved that environment and enjoyed it for several years.</p>

  <p>In a good year, so called when a winter snowpack was above average, Sawmill Gulch ran water from a spring just above the old sawmill site. The main spring was in an oval park at the head of the gulch but disappeared in slide rock before appearing near the mouth of the gulch. The spring afforded water for a "spring house" that kept dairy products cool and filled half a wooden barrel that had formerly been a whiskey keg. The half barrel had a small iron pipe running from a rocked-in sump to nestle in the oval where the bung had been and direct a stream containing silt and whatever critters could crawl into the sump to fill the barrel for drinking water and the always full tea kettle on the Home Comfort range for hand washing. We bathed in the Rio Grande on warm days, not at all in cold weather.</p>

  <p>I don't know which uncle to credit for acquainting us with horse hair snakes but after seeing the first one slithering around in the silt at the bottom of the barrel we were hooked. We were told only fresh hairs jerked from the mane of a horse, not cut, could become snakes. We believed everything our uncles told us. They were older, they were wiser and much of their lore came from the mouth of Garnier Gulch where Mr. Barber operated a distillery. After a visit to Mr. Barber's still we noticed improvements in their tales, though their eyes became bloodshot and they lost control of their legs for short periods. Grandmother Lane took a dim view of the source of their knowledge and they missed many meals in a recovery period far from her kitchen.</p>

  <p>As I remember, it took two weeks or longer to transform a horse hair into a snake. The best time to pluck hair from a horses mane, we learned from dodging hooves, was when they were hitched to a wagon. They resented losing these long hairs and rattled both single trees and the doubletree with shod hooves when we got greedy. The uncle told us the snakes could never be taken from the barrel or they would bite us and then die.</p>

  <p>That information could have been left unsaid, I wouldn't have touched one with a ten foot pole.</p>

  <p>We checked from the first day of immersion to see any signs of movement and when we weren't doing chores we were on our knees peering into the barrel. Never have I been as excited as the day I saw my very own snake slowly form an "S", straighten and do it again. I had the grey hairs from the horse named Shorty and Charlotte had brown hairs from the bay horse, Chub. There was no doubt in our infantile minds that we had performed a scientific feat equal to Mr. Barber's magic of drawing spirits from a tub of sour mash when we saw our snakes writhing from one side to the other in the bottom of the barrel.</p>

  <p>In trying to explain the complexities of our experiment to my teacher that fall she gently led me through the stages of evolving parasites attaching to the hairs to eventually make it seem that they were alive. After losing Santa Claus two years earlier this was far too much for a small boy to accept.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 2. "A homestead of 160 acres" gives the Lane ranch's approximate size; John's grandparents Charles and Maude Lane are elsewhere on this site.</em></p>
</section>

