<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="working-stiffs">Working Stiffs</h2>

  <p>In the year 1937, when the school bell was muted in May, I went on full time employment at a tourist resort owned and operated by Billy and Loraine Wills in Hinsdale County. This was my first summer away from my grandparents at the Lane ranch and was a serious adjustment, but with a salary of one dollar per week plus room and board an up and coming lad couldn't turn it down. Harold McCaulley, son of our shoe cobbler Dan McCaulley, was older and hired by Billy to help in pack trips to Lost Lakes to gather trout eggs in spawning season and as a fishing adventure for tourists. I was the chore boy. I carried wood to the cabins to keep the bins full and I carried trash back, helped the tourists catch fish and, occasionally, was rewarded with a horseback trip to the lakes. Harold and I bunked together in a small cabin but we ate all meals in Loraine's kitchen.</p>

  <p>It was an exciting summer and I saved the sum of $19.00 which my father invested for me in postal savings. This was the start of a savings program that enabled me to finance our first mining venture with $2,100.00 in the year 1949. I know, I know, that is a pittance in today's dollars but in 1949 a compressor and drill could be rented for $125.00 per month, used rail and pipe purchased for $20.00 per ton and 10&cent; per ft. respectively, and a 50-pound box of dynamite for less than the price of two bags of potato chips today.</p>

  <p>The $2,100.00 went fast even coupled with donations from various sources and failure was a bitter pill to swallow, however we profit in knowledge with every failure and if we won with each gamble life would become very boring. I can assure the readers I have rarely been bored in my mining career. I tried to get experienced help, not always possible on a shoestring budget, but I found that Creede had some dependable and ambitious young workers willing to listen and to learn. In 1954 I hired Paul Hosselkus to help tram ore from the Holy Moses #2 tunnel. Paul was just 18 years old and had lost part of a hand in a blasting cap mishap several years before. It would seem to follow that Paul would want nothing to do with mines or explosives but I never had cause to regret hiring him. Sick or hurting he was on the job on time, had no apparent fear of the hole and performed like a veteran. Unfortunately I was at the end of my rope, financially, so the job was short-lived and I couldn't fully compensate him for his ambition and loyalty.</p>

  <p>In the year 1969, financing came through via Allan and Clara Phipps and The Colorado Fuel, Iron and Steel Company to form Gateway Access Corporation in preparation of driving a 2,500' tunnel northwest in Nelson Mountain from the former Midwest property and 1920s Colewood tunnel. Experienced miners were in short supply due to the entry of Homestake Mining Company in our area and I was getting panicky when a young fellow drove up to the tunnel site in a red Ford pickup with New Mexico license plates. I had blasted to solid rock and was capping square sets for the portal when he got out, looked the place over, and asked if I was hiring. He was skinny as a rail and I thought would never be able to cut the mustard using heavy drills and 200 pound lengths of rail. He could sense my hesitation and told me he had been working in uranium mines and knew he would fit in. He had his wife and two small kids in the truck, looked me square in the eye, and added, "I need the work."</p>

  <p>That was my introduction to James Morrow who proved to be an exceptional worker and common sense miner. His parents had a gas station and store in Poncha Springs so he had roots in Colorado.</p>

  <p>Jim and I drove the tunnel 100 feet before the next top hand was hired. That was Michael "Mahone" McClure, a home town lad who came from a mining family and fit in with no problems. Teamed with Jim Morrow, Mike picked up the rhythm we had set in advancing 7 1/2 feet per shift and the tunnel went like clockwork with expenses well in a range that would complete the tunnel with extra for drilling core holes.</p>

  <p>Jim and Mike liked their beer and the fumes were overpowering in the early morning hours. I learned to judge the degree of their competence or incompetence as the compressor was warming up and we sometimes shoveled snow or stacked timber for an hour in cold temperatures before I felt safe in escorting them underground. We can all be thankful that James Morrow and "Mahone" McClure weren't with the British troops in the Revolutionary War. General Washington's troops, in order to save powder and shot, were commanded to not shoot until they saw the whites of the enemy's eyes. Jim and Mike would have waded right through the colonial soldiers as in all the time I worked with them I never saw the whites of their eyes.</p>

  <p>Creede is in good hands. With historians like Jan Jacobs and entrepreneurs like Ken Wyley to maintain the flavor with publications and staging mining contests the people who gave it a special aura will not be forgotten.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 5 (bordered as a boxed page in the printed book). "Capping square sets" is printed as shown, likely "dapping" or "tamping" in the original miner's usage; kept as printed.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="double-heart-failure">Double Heart Failure</h2>

  <p>Bryan, Tommy and Bernie were having a few drinks and swapping stories in a local bar when a message was relayed to Bryan, County Coroner, that an elderly lady had passed away in a suburb of Creede and his services were needed. They were all acquainted with the family and assumed there was no urgency so Bryan called a mortuary in the valley to send up a hearse. There would be at least an hour's wait for the vehicle to transport the body in a nighttime drive over dirt roads and family or friends would be at the home to comfort the grieving husband.</p>

  <p>They continued drinking with Bernie who was just finishing a long winded tale of South Pacific female islanders with sailors on leave when a pair of headlights rounded a lower corner and headed slowly up main street. Bryan left to stop the gentleman then returned to ask Tommy to come along and help.</p>

  <p>It had been nearly 90 minutes since the death message had been delivered. The lady was obviously far gone as Bryan searched for vital signs and Tommy sat in the kitchen offering condolences and help to the husband. Bryan walked into the kitchen after his examination and pulled a rickety chair near the table to fill out the necessary forms with the help of the husband. Where he was to fill in "Cause of Death" he wrote "heart failure coupled with old age". Heart failure was a staple of the times and covered everything but gunshot and stab wounds.</p>

  <p>The hearse was a modified 1938 Ford station wagon with little to offer other than the stretcher holding the body. Bryan and Tommy were squatted on either side of the stretcher for the ride back to the bar and Tommy was chiding Bryan about his choice of words about cause of death, "I noticed you put down heart failure coupled with old age. Did you think coupling with her old husband caused heart failure?"</p>

  <p>Bryan was laughing at Tommy's question when the lady suddenly sat up and asked, "Where are you taking me?" They nearly jumped out of their skin and were totally speechless. As suddenly as she had sat up her eyes rolled back and she collapsed with one final gasp and quiver. The coroner and his assistant were greatly relieved.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 7 (bordered as a boxed page). No surnames given for Bryan, Tommy, or Bernie.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="hell-cats-of-a-different-breed">Hell Cats of a Different Breed</h2>

  <p>There were ranchers who fit well in Key LeZotte's hell cats and she ghosts category. The Dabneys, Emmett and John, had ranches near Spring and Clear Creeks and in the spring moved cattle from the corrals at Wason, where they were hauled in on the D&amp;RG Railroad, to summer pasture near Spring Creek Pass. They usually camped a night or two at Wason Ranch to have everything in order before the drive began and enjoyed spirited night life in Creede.</p>

  <p>Bill Lewis had a tin covered shed behind the Mercantile building where he kept grain and salt for both cattlemen and sheepmen. There was a hitching rail just north of the shed for horses and Sis Parson's Beer and Billiards Parlor on the corner of main street was less than a half block away. Roy Phillips bought and ran the business after Sis died and he also catered to cowboys.</p>

  <p>Near midnight one night, as the drovers were leaving Creede, Emmett decided to practice steer roping on an outhouse that sat near the road with a path to an occupied home. He fashioned a beautiful, wide loop, twirled it over his head a couple of times and let go. His aim was perfect and when the loop dropped over the slanted roof he half hitched the rope to the pommel of his saddle and spurred his horse. The biffy dropped with a thud and he dragged it several yards before galloping back to retrieve his lasso.</p>

  <p>Unfortunately there was a lady inside on a late night run and she was screaming at the top of her lungs. The cowboys stood the building upright, rescued the fair damsel and escorted her to her residence. She was both frightened and mad. The next day she was still mad and Emmett was summoned into municipal court where he was fined $25.00 and costs.</p>

  <p>Both Emmett and John became miners in later years during winters in Creede. Emmett was part owner of the Midwest Mine in the early 1940s where he and John Van Buskirk tunneled by hand. John worked with George Logue in an inclined raise of the Amethyst mine in 1947-48 near a stope where I was working.</p>

  <p>When Emmett was in failing health in the Monte Vista Community Hospital Inez and I dropped in to see how he was coping in confinement. There was little change in this grand old man, he told stories, laughed and insisted on walking with us to the door. He was clad in his western hat, cowboy boots and a hospital gown. His bare butt exposed with every step.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 7 (bordered as a boxed page). Emmett and John Dabney and Roy Phillips are not otherwise identified beyond what is given here.</em></p>
</section>

