<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="the-bullet-belt">The Bullet Belt</h2>

  <p>In the year 1934 my father drove Sheriff William Orthen to a National Sheriff's Convention in Denver where he was presented with a Colt .45 caliber revolver in recognition for his service and being the oldest active sheriff in the nation.</p>

  <p>Bill had a pearl handled .38 caliber revolver which he felt was all the firepower he needed and gave the new revolver to Dad. This made two revolvers to grace our household. The other was a .44 caliber with a heavy holster and bullet belt. They both hung in a closet off our kitchen where we three boys could look but not touch.</p>

  <p>Mom Jo meted out punishment for minor infractions but when it came to major crimes like lying, stealing or sassing a neighbor she spoke the dreaded words that turned our knees to jelly, "Your father will use the bullet belt on you when he gets home."</p>

  <p>Dad had a razor strop he used in the house if he was in a hurry but if Mom Jo had promised the "bullet belt" we went to the woodshed where he would remove the holster from the belt and order the chosen one, "Bend over the chopping block and hold your pants tight".</p>

  <p>If it was me I would be crying lustily before he had the holster off. He would hang the belt in front of my face and ask, "Are you ever going to do that again?"</p>

  <p>"No," I would blubber.</p>

  <p>"I can't hear you," he would say.</p>

  <p>"No!" I would shout.</p>

  <p>"I still can't hear you."</p>

  <p>I would scream, "No, no, no!"</p>

  <p>With that he would work the belt through the loop on the holster and I was free to return to the kitchen where Mom Jo would have tears in her eyes waiting to hold me and tell me she still loved me. Dad would step between us and warn her, "Let him remember this for a day or two."</p>

  <p>I never told Joel or Scooter that Dad hadn't really whipped me. They would ask how many times he laid it on and if any of the bullets had come out of the belt. It wasn't a real whipping if bullets didn't go flying from their pouches. I didn't offer anything over my sobs that might catch me in another lie.</p>

  <p>After all these years and remembering their cries I'm sure their treatment at the hands of our father, Bill Jackson, was exactly the same as mine but I have often wondered if our Mom Jo knew of his method of punishment. All she ever heard were the screams, "No, no, no!" and her tears were so genuine. Too, she always found a way to comfort us after a bullet belt thrashing and Dad was out of sight.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 9 (bordered as a boxed page). John's account makes clear the belt itself, not the bullets, was the instrument, and that his own crying was performed rather than real; whether his brothers' experience matched his own is left as his own supposition, not asserted as fact.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="the-lawbreakers">The Lawbreakers</h2>

  <p>My father, William T. "Billy" Jackson, was undersheriff to William Orthen for many years in the late 1920s and 1930s. Sheriff Orthen was in failing health and Dad assumed more and more of his duties until Mr. Orthen decided he had had enough suffering and ended his life with a single shot to his heart, late one night, on the steps of a Creede business house. There were many suicides in that period of Dad's service and each one took its toll on him. They were all friends and old timers. He never talked about the deaths when he returned but there was a period of a week or two, after being called out, when he liked to be alone and come to grips with the loss.</p>

  <p>I just ran across his 1918-1919 record of service in the United States Marine Corps. The honorable discharge reads "Character &ndash; excellent" and it made me very proud of this fellow who broke up a shooting war between two local moonshiners in 1932, arrested a few unsavory characters and flattened a fellow lawman with a left cross. He had his right arm broken cranking a Model T Ford, it had never healed right and the cop wasn't expecting a punch from left field. Dad stood only 5' 9" and never weighed over 145 pounds but he never flinched under pressure. The fellow lawman was Creede's town marshal, briefly. He was a big fellow, boisterous and hadn't endeared himself to the town council or the citizens. The council had asked him to resign, he thought Dad was behind the request and was giving him a tongue lashing when Dad decided enough was enough and fed him a knuckle sandwich that very effectively ended the quarrel.</p>

  <p>In 1933 a pair of farmers from the Saguache-Center area were peddling potatoes at a dollar per hundred pound sack in Creede. Vern and Eugene were well known in Creede, came to dances with their wives and were considered honorary citizens. This was the beginning of the great depression and money was tight so they also traded for anything of equal value. On this trip pickings were slim. On the way home, just past Blue Creek, a deer crossed the road in from of them and stopped on the upper bank to watch them drive by. They weren't willing to go home empty handed so they shot it. They were just loading it in their truck when the game warden, Clarence Goade of Alamosa, drove up with his Chesapeake retriever on one of his infrequent trips. They were caught red handed, returned to Creede for trial where they were fined and sentenced to ten days in jail.</p>

  <p>The town of Creede and Mineral County were also short of dollars then and Dad's arrests of perpetrators of minor crimes and tramps were kept out of court for the most part. He and Sheriff Orthen usually fed them one meal and escorted them out of the town limits with a stern warning to never return. A tramp or two wandered back but others were more than willing to continue on their way once seeing the poverty of the town and the condition of the jail.</p>

  <p>The arrest and jailing of Vern and Eugene was a different story and needed a different approach. Dad solved the dilemma by unlocking their cell at 8:00 each morning and going on to his county treasurer's post. Vern and Eugene would then be on their own to walk from the jail to our house on the west mesa where Mom Jo would feed them breakfast. After breakfast they would roughhouse with Joel, Scooter and me for an hour or so before meandering back to jail. If they saw someone they knew they visited on their way ending up back in their cell with magazines and a Denver Post to read until Dad took them to the hotel for a free meal at 5:00 in the evening.</p>

  <p>Vern and Eugene never forgot this trust in them by Undersheriff Jackson and brought us a sack of potatoes with every visit to Creede, however they left Mr. Goade's deer alone thereafter. They have grandchildren in Saguache County at this date who may have heard them tell of their ten day vacation in Creede.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 9 (bordered as a boxed page). Sheriff William Orthen's suicide is also referenced from Get Grandpa Off The Thunder Mug's "Please Moo Before You Drop In," where John's father is the one who found him; Vern and Eugene are given no surnames.</em></p>
</section>

