<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="der-schwein-hunters">Der Schwein Hunters</h2>

  <p>Early in the year 1946 the occupation of Germany had become routine after initial blunders and our platoon sergeant, Company F, 332nd Engineers, was planning a hunt near Giessen. In the waning days of the shooting war then Cpl. Jim Kent and his squad had surprised some feeding wild schwein and he had since wanted to return and bag a boar.</p>

  <p>We were on our way. Sgt. Michael Kanuch of the motor pool, Sgt. Kent, Mess Sgt. Joe Hawkins, Cpl. Kenworthy and Privates Duncan and Jackson crammed into a Weapons Carrier carrying four bottles of Cognac, six rifles, sleeping bags and rations for two days. I couldn't believe the good fortune of Duncan and Jackson with lowly positions in Company F cadre to be invited on such an excursion until Sgt. Kent suggested we bring along all our cigarettes. Neither of us smoked but were allotted one carton per week and cigarettes bartered much better than occupation scrip or German Marks. We learned we would need many cigarettes, kippen as the Germans called them, before the weekend was over.</p>

  <p>Cpl. Kenworthy was moving us along the Autobahn at a steady 35 miles per hour clip with one bottle of Cognac drained when we came upon a Military Police Jeep near an overpass. One MP was on the overpass with a radio and carbine and another was at the rear of the Jeep waving us to a stop. Kenworthy began slowing when Kent yelled "Don't stop, give it hell and go around him." We swerved to the left narrowly missing the soldier as he stepped out thinking we were stopping, when we emerged from under the overpass Kenworthy had the vehicle floor-boarded. The canvas top was flapping against its frame, the engine was roaring and it was hard to make myself heard as I asked Kent why we didn't stop, he grabbed the flapping canvas and yelled, "Because we sort of borrowed the Weapons Carrier, have a drink and shut up."</p>

  <p>I was scared as hell but did as he directed, pulled a bottle from our gear and took a hefty slug. As I tipped it up I could see the MP on the overpass sighting the carbine and puffs at dust catching up with us on the roadside. I beat on the canvas and begged Kenworthy, "For God's sake stop, he's shooting at us!" I barely got the words out when the warm Cognac hit the pit of my stomach and came back up. As I was hanging onto the spare tire retching we came to a side road which Sgt. Kent ordered Kenworthy to take, throwing me to the other side on top of Duncan as the Jeep and the two MP's sped by on the Autobahn.</p>

  <p>There was no way we could return to the previous route so we followed a narrow dirt road to a river where an old man and a slender boy, perhaps fourteen years of age, were sitting by a makeshift ferry moored to a dock of sorts and held in place by pulleys attached to a cable securely anchored on both sides of the river. Another, smaller cable, ran from posts on either aide holding that cable above the water. Kanuch could speak passable German and negotiated with the old fellow to ferry us across for two packages of cigarettes. Kenworthy eased the truck onto the raft and, surprisingly it sank very little. The old gentleman gave each of us slotted clubs and showed us how to hook the slots over the cable and pull to propel the raft to the other side. It went smoothly and within an hour we were heading east on another narrow road that Sgt. Kent assured us would lead to safety and good hunting though by this time he was drunk and not sure where we were.</p>

  <p>We rounded a curve where two middle-aged Germans were cleaning an irrigation ditch. Kent ordered Kenworthy to stop and he and Kanuch walked back to the men. With much arm waving and excited ja ja's from the Germans, Kanuch determined we were in schwein country and the gentlemen would guide us. For two packages of Duncan's and Jackson's cigarettes they would put us on stands and they would drive the schwein to us. They took us to the end of what appeared to be about ten acres of heavy brush, spaced us along the sides and far end, and cautioned us to be careful and not fire in their direction. They told Sgt. Kanuch to be patient because they had to sneak to the other end and it would take much time to run the schwein our way. We waited...and waited, ... and waited. By dusk it was apparent we had been duped, all we could do was try to find a spot to camp with fresh water and a little wood to start a fire and warm up.</p>

  <p>We were late risers. The Cognac had served us well, we all had hangovers and the thoughts of returning to a possible court martial didn't help at all but we gathered ourselves together, loaded the Weapons Carrier and were back at the ferry just after noon. The old fellow and the boy were on the far bank lazing in the sun. Kent hollered at them and motioned for them to bring the raft over to our side. The old man held the boy by the shoulders as he spoke to him rapidly and the boy then slid into a small rowboat and rowed to us. The current swept him in an oval pattern but he expertly let it help as he turned the craft. I grabbed the prow to beach it and Kanuch walked down to talk to him. The boy laughed as Kanuch began assembling words to get our message across. "I speak English," the boy advised and went on, "That is my grossfader (grandfather) and he would like six packages of cigarettes before we can bring the ferry."</p>

  <p>Kanuch passed the request on to Kent who was still nursing a headache. "Tell him to go to hell," Kent barked, "he's only going to get two packs."</p>

  <p>The boy rowed back to his grandfather and we could see them talking animatedly with the boy laughing and pointing our way. He climbed in the boat again and crossed in even less time than before. "Mein grossfader said to tell you he has already been to hell because of soldaten (soldiers) like you and he must have six packages of kippen because of the extra weight of all the schwein that you killed." Sgt. Kent was livid, the veins in his neck bulged, his bloodshot eyes wanted to leave their sockets and he was choking on the swear words jammed together on his tongue as he remembered the German farmers who had promised to chase "beau coup" schwein to us. They must have passed the word about the great American schwein hunters. The boy opened the pockets of his jacket to receive the cigarettes and looking directly at Sgt. Kent said, "My grandfather wants you to hurry because the French like American lorries (trucks) and they," he began giggling, corrected himself, "will have a patrol here soon." Kent was reduced to a pitiful groan. When we crossed the river we had entered the French occupation zone and if they seized the Weapons Carrier we would really be in hot water.</p>

  <p>I never learned what strings were pulled to keep us out of the stockade when we were finally back with our unit but I did ask Sgt. Kent why he didn't tell us we were in a stolen Weapons Carrier. He answered, "You probably wouldn't have gone with us if you had known and we needed your cigarettes".</p>

  <p>We went on several legal hunts after the first escapade and Sgt. Kent killed his boar near the town of Giessen where we had initially headed. I believe we all developed a healthy respect for the old Ferrymaster and his grandson. It took a lot of courage to stand up to a group of the former enemy armed with rifles and win a token victory.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 5, out of sequence with "Working Stiffs" and "Double Heart Failure" and printed before them in the book despite the higher printed page number; kept in the book's own order. "Aide" for "side" is printed as shown.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="hero-for-a-day">Hero For A Day</h2>

  <p>We were the entire cadre of the 1907th L.S. Company overseeing the disposition of 124 German prisoners of war. 1st Lt. Richard O'Neill from a midwestern state and PFC John Jackson from Western Colorado, assigned to the 332nd Engineers, attached to the 1907th because Lt. O'Neill had fractured his right hand knuckles in a disagreement with another officer and was doing penance and because I could use a typewriter and was able to fill out forms.</p>

  <p>Lt. O'Neill had been married shortly before he enlisted in the Army. He was smart, dedicated and rose to the rank of 2nd Lieutenant when his unit was surrounded by German troops in the "Battle of the Bulge" and subsequently captured. As a prisoner of war all he dreamed of was his prim, pretty wife and home cooking.</p>

  <p>The war ended, he survived intact and was among thousands with enough points to be returned to the states for discharge and not shifted to the Pacific theater where battles with the Japanese were still raging. He arrived in his old home town with several other heroes on a train that whistled and tooted in celebration as the steam engine edged the coaches to a platform at the railroad station.</p>

  <p>The crowd cheered as the veterans of war in Europe stepped out with their barracks bags shouldered. Lt. O'Neill spotted his wife and in-laws in the first row and made for them. After a short speech by the mayor they were free to go.</p>

  <p>The father-in-law drove them home in his 1940 Chevrolet griping all the way about the hard times they endured as the war progressed. Recapped tires, gasoline and food rationing and so little sugar he had to stop using it in his coffee. Dick was properly sympathetic as he marveled at his good fortune to be back with his wife after two years of dodging shrapnel from artillery bombardments and bullets from machine guns and rifles.</p>

  <p>The in-laws' home was a two story affair with the guest bedroom, which Dick and Heather would share, directly over the old folks' bedroom. He unpacked his bag, in the bedroom as the ladies prepared the evening meal. Near the center of his accumulation he had stored the only war mementoes he was able to glom onto in their rush to relocation centers and assignment to troop transports headed home. He pulled out the German helmet and bayonet to show his father-in-law who was still trying to impress on Dick how the home folks had suffered, too, and later he would show him all the war bonds they had purchased to help the united effort. Finally, around 11:00 P.M., Dick was able to get his bride in bed. He snuggled to her, ran a hand through her long blonde hair, and made a suggestion. She pulled away and said, "Oh honey, we can't do anything here, Mother would hear us. Tomorrow you can look for a job and then we can get a house or apartment of our own and start a family."</p>

  <p>Lt. Richard O'Neill was doing his best to understand her reasoning as he mulled over the other events surrounding his homecoming when a neighbor's dog began barking. He watched the hour hand of a windup clock on the dresser creep around to 3:00 A.M. without a letup by the dog. He could no longer cope with his many frustrations. He vaulted out of bed, grabbed the bayonet and tore out of the house. It took several minutes to corner the dog and he was oblivious to lights blossoming in windows of houses nearby as he ran the bayonet through the dog's yapper and silenced him. He turned from the still quivering dog, nude save for his shorts and a bloody bayonet in his hand, to face two very nervous policemen with flashlights and drawn guns who had been called at the height of the commotion.</p>

  <p>His in-laws demanded his wife divorce him, the neighbors insisted he be jailed for killing their dog and his wife begged him to get psychiatric help. He thought it all over as he was being booked with everyone offering advice before he was led to a cell. He re-enlisted the next day and here we were, a year later, resting in a Jeep after a prisoners' check as he recounted his triumphant homecoming followed by banishment and divorce. He wondered if things might have gone differently if they had rented a hotel room instead of going to the in-laws' home, laughed and added, "No, I never did like that overbearing bastard and I don't think we could have ever got along."</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 11 (bordered as a boxed page). John names himself among the 1907th's cadre and tells the story as Lt. O'Neill's own, recounted to him afterward; the dog's killing is left exactly as told.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="hell-cats">Hell Cats</h2>

  <p>It was a Saturday night in 1949 when industry was thriving in Mineral County and its proceeds supporting three bars in Creede. One major mine producing steadily, two smaller mines shipping, on occasion, and three sawmills stacking up lumber and mine props. We worked six days per week and Saturday night was for socializing with a day off to recuperate before returning to the dark holes or timbered hillsides on Monday.</p>

  <p>We were in the Rainbow Bar and Grill where seeds of unrest were most apt to sprout with loggers and miners tearing up the turf and each other. If the patrons were in a peaceful mood the owners provided a slam bang dragout with much swearing, and blood letting. "The Great White Ape", female half of the partnership, was belligerent enough when sober, when drunk she was as likely to spray the place with slugs from a .25 caliber pistol as to scratch and bite anyone within range.</p>

  <p>On this night it looked like we might reach "drink up time" at 2:00 A. M. without a scuffle when a long-legged redhead thought her husband was paying too much attention to a logger's wife. It began with a few words of warning, a spilled drink and a bump or two then erupted into full scale war. I had learned to get my back against a wall after being coldcocked in a near riot one night by a round house punch thrown from somewhere to my right. I was standing with my elbows on the bar watching Bryan Beck and Pug Sutherland mix it up on one side while George Logue and a spud roller were trading punches on the other side. The next thing I knew I was sprawled near Bryan and Pug who were now wrestling and grunting by the rail. With people scattering to make room for the redhead and the brunette, Woody Wintz and I took a stand near the end of the bar where we could cover each other.</p>

  <p>This wasn't your ordinary screaming, hair pulling contest. These gals had several bouts behind them with their husbands and flirting bar gals, they were throwing punches like pros.</p>

  <p>The floor between the booths and stools didn't have enough square feet for the combatants and they ended up in the street with divided rooting sections, miners for the redhead and loggers for the brunette. The redhead had seriously underestimated the strength and endurance of the brunette. Within minutes she was on her back with smeared mascara and trickles of blood working into her curls from each nostril. The loggers won that one, but wait until next week.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 11 (bordered as a boxed page). No surnames given for the redhead, the brunette, or "The Great White Ape"; Bryan Beck, Pug Sutherland, George Logue, and Woody Wintz are named.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="survival-of-the-fittest">Survival of the Fittest</h2>

  <p>When visited by friends we haven't seen for years invariably, one will mention how lucky I am that Inez hasn't killed me. It isn't all luck. Much of my self preservation is due to calculated psychological survival. I'm not sure what that means, either, but it sounds technical so I slipped it in. I must admit I have tempted her many times and the only valid reason she hasn't dispatched me to eternal sleep is because I let my insurance policy lapse.</p>

  <p>In 1951 I bought her a brand new .30-.30 caliber hunting rifle so she could join me in cold, wet hunting forays around Creede. She coped well most of the time and seemed to enjoy being out in the wild save for one trip that began in East Willow Creek thence up Third Gulch to a series of parks that ended near Half Moon Pass with the return hike via Farmer's Creek to Wason Ranch. Our footprints covered about twelve miles and if I hadn't taken her rifle the last three miles I'm sure she would have plugged me and then told the sheriff she thought I was an elk. She, alternately, threw rocks at me and cried until she was too tired to pick up a rock and too dry to wring out a tear. The next year she guided herself.</p>

  <p>A few years later I arrived home at a late hour in less than honorable condition after a sterling performance by the "Upper Willow Creek Glee and Choir Club" conducted by Newt Turnquist, of a Denver mine equipment firm, until he was unable to lift the baton and direction was taken over by Eugene D. McClure, manager of Tomkins Hardware Company. When a member finishes such exhausting rehearsals the last thing he wants to hear is a carping wife reminding him of his failures as a husband.</p>

  <p>I loaded the rifle, levered a shell in the chamber and told her, "Okay, you get the first shot and you had better make it a good one!" She dissolved in tears and the crisis was over.</p>

  <p>See! That's what "calculated psychological survival" means. If I had just known of the curative powers of jalape&ntilde;o peppers during those years we would have had less problems. She has joined me in eating one pepper at lunch each day. A mild pepper will leave her speechless for 30 minutes, a pepper spouting fire from both ends mutes her for a full two hours.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 12. Told throughout as comic exaggeration of John's marriage to Inez, including the loaded-rifle anecdote; kept as printed.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="graveyard-poker">Graveyard Poker</h2>

  <div class="source-doc-poem">
    <p>The moon was full and the tidal pull<br>
    made the timbers creak and groan<br>
    in the Last Chance shaft where a natural draft<br>
    brought forth a quavering moan</p>

    <p>Near the end of our shift in a square set drift<br>
    mucked out, timbered and clean<br>
    the rail spiked true for the day shift crew<br>
    Frick Nelson, Gus Prince and Al Dean</p>

    <p>We learned at the station of a minor complication<br>
    when Red belled for a lift to the collar<br>
    the skip got hung when a guide rail sprung<br>
    the overtime would gain us a dollar</p>

    <p>With nothing to do 'til the maintenance crew<br>
    got everything back on track<br>
    Bill says "What the heck" and pulls out a deck<br>
    "a little poker will take up the slack"</p>

    <p>A make shift table on top of old cable<br>
    was level enough for a deal<br>
    he shuffled the cards for his mining pards<br>
    though he knew each one by feel</p>

    <p>A hair raising feeling as Bill started dealing<br>
    made me turn to a ghostly arrival<br>
    an eerie apparition with roots in perdition<br>
    searching for means of survival</p>

    <p>A rumbling cough dislodged a thick broth<br>
    of silica dust and green phlegm<br>
    that he spat toward the wall, rolled into a ball<br>
    and quivered in silent requiem</p>

    <p>I fingered three Jacks and tried to relax<br>
    from a haunting premonition<br>
    things wouldn't go right on this fateful night<br>
    with this refugee from perdition</p>

    <p>Relieved when I saw, two Kings on the draw<br>
    a full house the very first caper<br>
    I scribbled I O U, ten dollars would be due<br>
    the bearer of this slip of paper</p>

    <p>Red says "I'm through" and Bill folded, too<br>
    as the ghost slowly pushed in his poke<br>
    a prospector's pride, gold gleaming inside<br>
    this wraith was going for broke</p>

    <p>My senses went numb as the stub of a thumb<br>
    fanned his cards to see what he drew<br>
    he whispered "I'll call if it's winner take all<br>
    and your soul goes with the I O U"</p>

    <p>It was then I remembered a miner dismembered<br>
    at this very shaft, I was told<br>
    crushed by a skip that fell from the lip<br>
    I shuddered and said, "I'll fold"</p>
  </div>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 12 (bordered as a boxed page). A closing poem in the ghost-story tradition of the book's title; no note in the text on which mine's Last Chance shaft is meant.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="ed-and-cherokee">Ed and Cherokee</h2>

  <p>Ed McCargish was not a hell cat or a she ghost in the sense that Key LeZotte perceived such but he was different. Ed had been a coal miner in Trinidad before coming to Creede and was pretty much a loner for the first couple of years working in mines controlled by Emperius Mining Company.</p>

  <p>I first worked with Ed in the year 1947 when he timbered for me in an open stope. Big timbers were solidly wedged and I felt comfortable drilling from them, less comfortable when we ate our lunch at noon break. Ed had a large, purplish growth on the wrist of his right hand. I didn't ask about it though I did wonder why he didn't have it removed. Every lunch period the main topics of conversation revolved around the Pope and Priests and in a most unfavorable light. When he felt we were well enough acquainted, the story unfolded of his broken marriage allegedly fostered by a Catholic Priest. He had tried to kill the fellow and injured his wrist therefore the growth and he would keep it to constantly remind him of his wife's infidelity and the Priest. The visits disturbed me and I was relieved when Ed replaced Paul Weaver on the hoist serving our level.</p>

  <p>The old Irishman was a good worker and a fair poet. He published a small booklet of his rhymes and through it had somewhat of a fan club in Creede which, in turn, directed his attention from Priests and the current Pope so we had a much better relationship and I hired him to help in hoisting and timbering at the Phoenix mine. He reeked of garlic that he constantly nibbled and a couple of the men complained of the smell, however he was punctual and never missed a shift.</p>

  <p>I first picked up Art Davis each morning then drove by the Creede Transportation Company garage where Ed would be waiting on the step under cover of an overhanging roof. It was winter, early 1952, and just as Ed stepped on the running board of the truck his other foot slipped on ice and he hit on the back of his head with a sickening thud. I ran around the truck to help Art get him on his feet sure he would have a concussion and would need medical help. He had on a stocking cap under his old, black felt hat that partially cushioned his fall and when I dusted the snow off his hat and asked him how he felt he climbed in the truck without answering.</p>

  <p>That ride to the mine will never be forgotten. He let loose with interconnected swear words that formed a satisfactory description of Priests and Popes, in his opinion, for the first two miles then he launched into a tirade about the F. B. I. and J. Edgar Hoover who had his house bugged and watched his every move. I had made up my mind, by this time, to take the rest of the crew to the mine and return Ed to town for a visit with Agnes Egan, a registered nurse, and very likely a hospital stay. When we reached the mine Art stepped out first followed by Ed who grabbed his lunch pail and started for the dry room still ranting and raving. He had barely cleared the rail leading to the portal when he, again, challenged a patch of ice with the same results, a sharp crack to the back of his head that resounded in the thin morning air. He was noticeably dazed, sat for a few seconds and, as I helped him to his feet, said, "I must be getting old and clumsy." He didn't remember the first fall nor the ride to the mine and insisted on putting in his shift. He never again mentioned Priests, Popes or J. Edgar Hoover.</p>

  <p>Only a few years before this session with Ed I was an ore hauler for Creede Transportation Company on my way to the Amethyst Mine with a passenger who called himself "Cherokee". He was quite drunk, noisy and waving his arms from window to window as he sprayed the dashboard and my right cheek with tobacco juice. He looked wild eyed at me and confided, "I'm really an F.B.I. agent and I've been working undercover here. You'd better watch your step because you're on the list." I was a seventeen year old smartalec kid and I had violated laws in various ways but I was certain no violation was serious enough to merit being on a F.B.I. list.</p>

  <p>Ed McCargish had a different personality that worried me but he never scared me. Old Cherokee scared the hell out of me and I believe he fit well in Key LeZotte's category of "Hell Cats and She Ghosts".</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 13. Ed's head injuries and paranoid talk of the FBI are told as separate, back-to-back falls; John draws no medical conclusion beyond his own worry, and none is added here.</em></p>
</section>

<section class="source-doc-transcript">
  <h2 id="curiodditie-s">Curiodditie s</h2>

  <p>Most of this prattle is being written in February and March, 2001, to combat cabin fever. If I'm sitting at my desk with my brow furrowed Inez believes I am deep in thought and leaves me pretty much alone. One time when she checked she found the furrowed brow was caused by a glassful of prune juice that worked without warning. She approaches me carefully now with her nostrils flared.</p>

  <p>Creede made Ripley's "Believe It Or Not" in 1944 as you have noted. Mineral County's elective offices paid very little in its juvenile years and those in office held other jobs, simultaneously, to make ends meet. My dad was treasurer of the county, clerk of the district court and a butcher for A. H. Major which, combined, brought in $130.00 monthly, equal to a male teacher's salary. Johnny Weaver, county assessor, was a foreman at the Commodore mine during his term in office. Bill Swinehart, county sheriff following William Orthen, worked at both mines and mill for a living wage. Herbert Barnhart was the only county officer, long time county clerk, who didn't have additional employment at that time.</p>

  <p>Of interest and I have always thought a curioddity, we had two families of Weavers in the county. Arthur and Minnie Weaver owned a resort, Antelope Park, and had four children, Stanley, Ivan, Woodrow and Minnie. They were all Republicans. Thomas and Mary Weaver also had three boys and a girl, John, Billy, Paul and Mary. They were all Democrats. Both Ivan Weaver and Billy Weaver were town electricians in different periods. I worked for Billy one summer replacing power poles and also worked with Paul at the Amethyst mine. I was around all but Mary, who had moved to Pueblo, and could never detect the political difference. Under the skin I believe we are all Americans.</p>

  <p>The following isn't exactly a curioddity but I have no other place to put it without changing the index. A word battle erupted between a bar owner and his wife late one night as she was totalling the day's receipts and stuffing them in a money bag that also contained a .25 caliber pistol. He needed a little cash and she wanted to hold all for deposit. It became more than a word battle with a punch or two thrown when she grabbed the pistol and aimed it at his midsection. A dear friend of mine stepped between them and asked her to put the gun down. She responded by shooting him. The slug entered by his belly button and exited near his left kidney missing her husband completely. The moral to the story is: If you must take sides in a domestic dispute stand firmly "behind" the husband.</p>

  <p class="source-doc-note"><em>Printed page 13. "Some seventy years age" elsewhere in the book and "curioddity"/"Curiodditie s" here are printed as shown; this story's opening line, dating the writing to February and March 2001, is the firmest date given anywhere in the book. Mineral County's Weaver families are two of several same-surname, unrelated households noted across John's books.</em></p>
</section>
